\title{The Real Line Is Second-Countable}
\taxon{Example}
\p{
    In the Euclidean real line, let
    ##{
        \mathcal{B}
        =\{(p,q)\mid p,q\in\mathbb{Q},\;p<q\}.
    }
    This collection is countable because #{\mathbb{Q}\times\mathbb{Q}} is
    countable. It is a basis: whenever #{U} is open and #{x\in U}, there is an
    interval #{(a,b)} with #{x\in(a,b)\subseteq U}; density of the rationals
    gives #{p,q\in\mathbb{Q}} with
    ##{
        a<p<x<q<b.
    }
    Thus #{x\in(p,q)\subseteq U}. Hence #{\mathbb{R}} is second-countable and,
    by [second-countability implies first-countability](thm-001M), also
    first-countable.
}
